{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794390638,"nonce":"EPwgaGuIiP1UeSvgD1Kdrcl+UCk=","title":"Wasm support: what works as an Ash side module and what does not yet","message":"The counter fixture now builds as an Ash wasm side module (`dylink.0`,\n`wasm32-wasip1`) and `tests/counter/run.sh wasm \u003cash\u003e` passes under\n`ash run`: handles, finalizers, text and bytes, failures thrown to Haxe,\nfutures and `then`. The HashLink binding and `hxn-hl` needed no change; the\nlibrary sets `hl_abi::ProgramAllocator` and is linked from a static archive.\n\nKnown gaps, in rough order of how much they constrain a library author:\n\n- A panic ends the program. The side module recipe builds std with\n  `panic_abort`, so `catch_unwind` catches nothing and `Status::PANIC` does\n  not exist on wasm. Under plain WASI the first deliberate panic in\n  hxn-abi's own unit tests aborts the test binary the same way. Building with\n  `panic_unwind` over wasm exception handling is untried, and Ash emits the\n  current exception encoding where rustc may still default to the legacy one.\n- No threads on `wasm32-wasip1`. A side module that calls\n  `std::thread::spawn` imports `pthread_create` and `pthread_detach`, which\n  Ash's runtime does not define, and the build is refused. hxn-future now\n  detects a wasm build without atomics: `spawn` runs its work before\n  returning and `wait` returns at once instead of blocking. So an operation\n  written with `spawn` is synchronous there.\n- Nothing settles a future from outside the module yet. In a browser the\n  result of real asynchronous work arrives in a host callback. `Completer`\n  works for that, but no library here imports a host function, and the\n  browser host has not been run at all.\n- `hxn.Future.await` polls with `Sys.sleep` while a wait returns pending.\n  That only makes progress in a build with fibers (`ASH_WASM_FIBERS=1`);\n  without them a future that is still pending hangs. Untested, because with\n  `spawn` running inline no future is ever pending on wasm today.\n- `wasm32-wasip1-threads` is untested. It needs the library's own `errno`\n  slot and `--shared-memory`, per Ash's `scripts/build_wasm_channel.sh`.\n- `ProgramAllocator` returns null for an alignment above 16, so a\n  `Handle\u003cT\u003e` of an over-aligned `T` cannot be allocated in a side module.\n- hxcpp to wasm through Emscripten is untried. The crates type-check for\n  `wasm32-unknown-emscripten`; `emcc` is not installed here.\n- The build is a recipe in run.sh: nightly with `rust-src`,\n  `-Z build-std=std,panic_abort`, PIC, and `rust-lld` with one `--export`\n  per primitive. It belongs in `hxn build`. Homebrew's `wasm-ld` 20.1.7 on\n  this machine fails to start against LLVM 21, which is why the script uses\n  the toolchain's `rust-lld`.\n- Size: the debug side module is about 7 MB. Ash's notes put a release\n  std-using library near 770 KB.\n\nThe scope document's artifact table has no wasm row. It needs one for the\nAsh side module, and a decision on whether hxcpp through Emscripten is in\nscope.","files":null}]}